From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 11:45:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 338/488] Prepare the V2 runner's step inputs with TileLang
 kernels

The runner's integer input preparation launched eleven Triton kernels
every step. Each is now a TileLang kernel with the Triton kernel's
contract, element for element, including which elements a launch writes
and which it leaves alone: block-table gather and slot mappings, staged
writes (single tensor and the fused multi-group writer), KV block
zeroing, prefill inputs, positions and sequence lengths, the
sampled/draft combine (with dead rows), logits index expansion, the
commit of sampled tokens (with request-boundary captures), DFlash input
preparation, and the DeepSeek V4.1 Engram lookback gather. Each call site
launches the TileLang kernel; the Triton kernels stay importable as the
tests' reference and are no longer launched.

The kernels live in vllm/v1/worker/gpu/tilelang_inputs.py (the lookback,
model code, in deepseek_v4_1/tilelang/lookback.py). Integer arithmetic
follows Triton's: C division and remainder, int32 products where Triton
multiplies int32 values, truncating stores. The dead-row survival takes
logf of the draft confidences and sums them in the warp scan's
Kogge-Stone order; the kernel compiles with -ftz=true, the flush-to-zero
path of libdevice's logf that Triton selects.

Where the Triton kernels looped serially, the ports spread the work: the
slot mappings cover each group's row in 1024-token blocks (a token finds
its request by binary search of the query starts), so the PAD fill and a
16K-token prefill chunk no longer run one program a group; the gather
covers only each group's 1024-column chunks of a row, in 16-byte moves
where the table allows; staged writes split long writes over blocks; the
commit reads the output-token counts of a request's distinct tokens
before writing any, so its increments no longer chain.

Preparation: each launch resolves its kernel through the key function the
owner's declaration uses, so a declared call and a launched call cannot
drift apart. The owners declare through DeepSeek V4.1's TileLang
preparation (the JitWarmupRegistry owner TILELANG) where the Triton
dispatchers registered their warmups: the runner's step kernels in
declare_step_kernels, now on every pipeline rank (a stage before the last
commits without query starts or output-token counts), every staged
tensor's write keyed by element type alone, the drafter's in its
register_warmups, the lookback in the model state. The worker compiles
them with the registry before memory profiling; after the cache freezes
an undeclared call raises KernelNotPreparedError by name. Outside a
collecting registry a declaration is a no-op, as a Triton dispatcher's
registration is.
